course: dictionary
lesson: 7650
title: Promenade: a path, a walk, a dance
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, so use the context to choose the right one. «Anyone who has eaten chips on a seaside promenade knows the gull that swoops down and snatches one right out of a child's hand.» The seaside and the chips tell you that a promenade is a wide path by the sea, where people walk.
explain: The same word can be a verb too. If people walk slowly and grandly in a park to enjoy themselves or to be seen, they promenade. Read the clues: is it a place, or something people are doing? That tells you whether it is a noun or a verb.
explain: Some meanings are only found in certain places or groups. In Britain people say «the prom» for the seaside path. In America a school «prom» is a formal dance, and the name comes from an old meaning of promenade. In square dancing, a promenade is a move where partners walk round the circle together. Check the clues each time.
fact: A promenade is a wide path by the sea, for walking along.
fact: «The prom» is a short, friendly way to say promenade, used in Britain, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland.
fact: As a verb, promenade means to walk slowly and grandly in public, to enjoy yourself or to be seen.
fact: The American school «prom» gets its name from an old word for a formal dance.
fact: «Gulls are bold and clever, and they have learned that people carry food.»
exercise: «Anyone who has eaten chips on a seaside promenade knows the gull that swoops down.» What is a promenade in this sentence? | A tall cliff with steps | *A wide path by the sea, for walking along | A shop that sells chips | A kind of bird
exercise: «Grandma likes to promenade through the park on Sunday afternoons, looking at the flowers.» What does promenade mean here? | To run very fast | *To walk slowly and grandly in public | To swim across a lake | To sleep under a tree
exercise: Ella's family went «on the prom» for ice cream. Where did they go? | *To the wide path by the sea | A big shop in town | A castle on a hill | A school dance
test: In old American English, a school «prom» was named after an old meaning of promenade. What meaning is that? | A square dance | A loud party in a field | A slow walk to the shops | *A formal dance
test: In square dancing, what is a promenade? | A loud shout | A type of song | *A move where partners walk round the circle together | A tall hat worn by the caller
exam: «Anyone who has eaten chips on a seaside promenade knows the gull that swoops down.» Which word best describes the promenade? | *A wide path by the sea | A slow dance in a hall | A shop | A kind of bird
